  "title": "Characterizing Activity of gata5 Candidate Cis-Regulatory Elements during Zebrafish Heart Development",
  "year": 2025,
  "abstract": "GATA4/5/6 transcription factors enable fate divergence in cardiopharyngeal progenitors by differentially regulating chromatin accessibility at cardiac and pharyngeal enhancers. The importance of GATA4/5/6 in cardiac development is well-known yet how their expression is regulated remains to be established. My thesis project involves identifying gata5 candidate cis-regulatory elements (cCREs) from scATAC-seq data and characterizing their activity during zebrafish development. All cCREs tested display cardiac related enhancer activity. In particular, gata5-CRE4 recapitulates some of gata5\u2019s early activity and mutants show reduced gata5 expression. A small percentage of gata5-CRE4 mutants develop abnormal cardiac morphology, establishing an important but non-essential role for gata5-CRE4. When Gata5/6 is knocked down, gata5 expression driven by gata5-CRE4 is insufficient to rescue heart development. A core region within gata5-CRE4 was determined to likely be responsible for its enhancer activity. Overall, the identification and characterization of gata5 cCREs will help elucidate how differential gata5 expression occurs for cardiopharyngeal fate divergence."
